{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 380 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "0b0dd2e97d8446dd71ac5cf6d19c0b516e23c803248e9195f15f464708051af5" :edb/db-before-t 379 :edb/db-after-t 380 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533692 50 #inst "2026-10-04T17:13:20.207Z" 13194139533692 true] [17592186124391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247264 13194139533692 true] [17592186124391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247271 13194139533692 true] [17592186124391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247278 13194139533692 true] [17592186124392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247285 13194139533692 true] [17592186124392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247292 13194139533692 true] [17592186247264 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247265 13194139533692 true] [17592186247264 1089 "q-108795" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247264 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533692 true] [17592186247264 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/def2afaad6d1f74ca3ac8833ed6e35efea1296b324ef5a14c2d9513983a63456.png)\n\nDetermine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)$ (shown above) is invertible." 13194139533692 true] [17592186247264 1113 #uuid "f9b1d4b5-ce4c-5ef1-a359-cd6d35ef33f8" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247264 1138 false 13194139533692 true] [17592186247264 1145 "First, notice that the domain of our function is $x\\in [-2,4].$\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ does not pass the horizontal line test on the interval $x\\in [-2,0].$ In this case, the horizontal line $y=-2$ intersects the graph at more than one point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/033b9e827caf4fc89d4b179c769f5115254c811953f93caf33d0959bfd063235.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in [0,4].$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/4c91aa95131758065b1d8befe7c69895dc51d2f984a04cc8f06932a32e552792.png)\n\nTherefore, the largest interval on which the function is invertible is:\n\n$$\nx\\in [0,4]\n$$" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247266 13194139533692 true] [17592186247265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247267 13194139533692 true] [17592186247265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247268 13194139533692 true] [17592186247265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247269 13194139533692 true] [17592186247265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247270 13194139533692 true] [17592186247265 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533692 true] [17592186247265 1041 "selection" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247265 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247267 13194139533692 true] [17592186247265 1110 #uuid "ad221038-ac8d-5361-bc3e-62a8aae5cac6" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247266 1034 "x\\in [-2,0]" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247266 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533692 true] [17592186247266 1111 #uuid "d4cc1890-c18d-5a13-a9ce-350ca623f0e6" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247267 1034 "x\\in [0,4]" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247267 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533692 true] [17592186247267 1111 #uuid "53f0ea70-840c-52a5-8f8e-7e1f6ff3a93b" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247268 1034 "x\\in (0,4)" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247268 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533692 true] [17592186247268 1111 #uuid "d117117c-35e4-5cb4-8557-ed472252206b" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247269 1034 "x\\in [-2,4]" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247269 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533692 true] [17592186247269 1111 #uuid "13fa0eb4-2662-5191-a558-3d224d6037d2" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247270 1034 "x\\in (-2,4)" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247270 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533692 true] [17592186247270 1111 #uuid "d826f68a-e6a8-5ed6-8a2e-5e486563dfcb" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247271 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247272 13194139533692 true] [17592186247271 1089 "q-36008" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247271 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533692 true] [17592186247271 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/279c3cd6084423d913186f4ba467ea50d0f794cf54773785777cb8841f5c2ca1.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-3,-1]$\n- $x\\in [-1,3]$\n- $x\\in [0,1]$" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247271 1113 #uuid "2527bbd8-ce59-54a7-8446-7823452ff16c" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247271 1138 false 13194139533692 true] [17592186247271 1145 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The given function is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-3,-1]$ and on $x\\in [0,1].$  Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/0388335095ed530ce07d278f82be5eb3bff14859db64b6e9ec47da27861bb657.png)\n\n    \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval. $x\\in [-1,3]$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/522b5661aaf9b7640c305b8cefd06f49cc8eed590f3ae736fc6720e6165bc3a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533692 true] [17592186247272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247273 13194139533692 true] [17592186247272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247274 13194139533692 true] [17592186247272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247275 13194139533692 true] [17592186247272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247276 13194139533692 true] [17592186247272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247277 13194139533692 true] [17592186247272 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533692 true] [17592186247272 1041 "selection" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247272 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247274 13194139533692 true] [17592186247272 1110 #uuid "896dcd52-2934-5d16-967a-67e5193ad3ae" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247273 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247273 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247273 1111 #uuid "76a62ba4-8d2b-5c2c-813f-55a188da8c2d" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247274 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247274 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247274 1111 #uuid "c0ce0c15-affc-5f3a-8003-35e6001668fe" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247275 1034 "I only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247275 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247275 1111 #uuid "36ef5ed4-1f1d-583b-8ec4-0ca44da0b84a" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247276 1034 "III only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247276 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247276 1111 #uuid "f14f122f-ac73-57fd-9b99-000f27d1e194" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247277 1034 "II only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247277 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247277 1111 #uuid "8a5d7ff8-6be9-52f9-a738-a92b9c9a3ff2" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247278 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247279 13194139533692 true] [17592186247278 1089 "q-36011" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247278 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533692 true] [17592186247278 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/b02c2e94e144935f0ff96846cd51b5a969fa8f884a7ca1d09abb231b7c945232.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible? \n\n- $x\\in (-4,-2)$\n- $x\\in [-2,3]$\n- $x\\in [-3,3]$" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247278 1113 #uuid "d650f0a9-fd5d-507e-a056-8206ec6f99b0" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247278 1138 false 13194139533692 true] [17592186247278 1145 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-4,-2)$ and $x\\in [-2,3].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/53fe88780b2ccb7522d2f1baa711162ec99300c2a2d4da29bba9ca532026cd07.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on  the interval $x\\in [-3,3]$  because some lines will  intersect the curve at more than one point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/63cee4fc05ab17a8e69d9bfae578fa83c24d73e4677065a54a33a25e0871e718.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533692 true] [17592186247279 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247280 13194139533692 true] [17592186247279 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247281 13194139533692 true] [17592186247279 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247282 13194139533692 true] [17592186247279 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247283 13194139533692 true] [17592186247279 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247284 13194139533692 true] [17592186247279 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533692 true] [17592186247279 1041 "selection" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247279 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247283 13194139533692 true] [17592186247279 1110 #uuid "bf31fb09-af3e-5bd4-8dbf-0959c7520f3a" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247280 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247280 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247280 1111 #uuid "7b82cedc-8826-5d7a-8049-bd7e75b056f1" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247281 1034 "I only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247281 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247281 1111 #uuid "db9e6792-fef2-58a4-ba35-7719d3d97ef6" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247282 1034 "III only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247282 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247282 1111 #uuid "9e196ccb-8c57-5e2a-9599-3601ac27e60e" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247283 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247283 1111 #uuid "a09ec9a7-8948-5881-9d7a-0d5559742687" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247284 1034 "II only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247284 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247284 1111 #uuid "c45c5c26-1b43-53c2-a227-4d0e3eee9613" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247285 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247286 13194139533692 true] [17592186247285 1089 "q-66483" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247285 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533692 true] [17592186247285 1100 "Given that $f(x)=1-x,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (0,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-2,2)$" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247285 1113 #uuid "1d69f632-8fdd-59a0-ac8c-5d011c3cdeaa" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247285 1138 false 13194139533692 true] [17592186247285 1145 "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $-1$ and $y$-intercept $1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/73d9277c040ae457ba6d96e162574edbeeef965d181fa6011ca4ab0e4644bd82.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ and $x\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533692 true] [17592186247286 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247287 13194139533692 true] [17592186247286 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247288 13194139533692 true] [17592186247286 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247289 13194139533692 true] [17592186247286 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247290 13194139533692 true] [17592186247286 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247291 13194139533692 true] [17592186247286 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533692 true] [17592186247286 1041 "selection" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247286 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247287 13194139533692 true] [17592186247286 1110 #uuid "fc1dde06-b0cb-5b44-8493-384e4ca0292c" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247287 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247287 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247287 1111 #uuid "2fc46fb9-f5fa-5a1b-9e20-b7d031a52ab6" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247288 1034 "I only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247288 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247288 1111 #uuid "c571ab9d-03e1-549f-af91-68e832ac65f5" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247289 1034 "III only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247289 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247289 1111 #uuid "6e39ae33-384f-5db3-95e7-98d3ff078d79" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247290 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247290 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247290 1111 #uuid "1695ddc5-efe0-57d6-83d2-1e4411875614" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247291 1034 "II only" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247291 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533692 true] [17592186247291 1111 #uuid "94f97e89-f832-5e56-b367-8a64a99a0eaa" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247292 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247293 13194139533692 true] [17592186247292 1089 "q-66679" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247292 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533692 true] [17592186247292 1100 "Given that $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n- $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-1,3]$" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247292 1113 #uuid "888d7ea8-2f15-562e-8a8d-c02982ae9999" 13194139533692 true] [17592186247292 1138 false 13194139533692 true] [17592186247292 1145 "The graph of the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Translate it by $1$ units to the right,  to get $y=(x-1)^{2}.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=(x-1)^{2}$  by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/f53a4f97166877085c90acc01e3f8d0c6ac7bb3f704bbfb792d747122d314013.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$ and $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/b088c8ed1974a7c259dd54f85aec7a124579981971babfe8dee0313ca23b6458.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,3].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/b3af13782a01213bb09fdeef38fbeb3fbca3f41348036e6786b4efa74a0166b4.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533692 true] [17592186247293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247294 13194139533692 true] [17592186247293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247295 13194139533692 true] [17592186247293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247296 13194139533692 true] [17592186247293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247297 13194139533692 true] [17592186247293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247298 13194139533692 true] [175921862472